{"name":"Raw Silk","hex":"#e1d9c7","fetched":"2026-10-12","looked":{"definitions":[{"term":"raw silk","whole":true,"parts":[{"part":"Noun","definitions":["Silk that has not been processed to remove sericin."]}],"link":"https://en.wiktionary.org/wiki/raw_silk"}],"entries":[{"title":"Raw Silk","blocks":[{"text":"Raw Silk was an American dance group which originated in New York. The original lineup comprised Jessica Cleaves, Tenita Jordan, and Sybil Thomas. The group released a single called \"Do It to the Music\" on West End Records, which peaked in the Top 10 on the US Billboard Hot Dance/Disco Club Play chart."}],"link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Raw_Silk"},{"title":"Raw Silk (film)","blocks":[{"text":"Raw Silk is a 1989 Australian film directed by Greg Dee. The plot concerns two barristers. It was a pilot for a TV series that did not eventuate and featured an early performance from Sophie Lee. It was a rare lead role for David Argue."}],"link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Raw_Silk_(film)"},{"title":"Noil","blocks":[{"text":"Noil refers to the short fibers that are removed during the combing process in spinning. These fibers are often then used for other purposes."}],"image":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/f/f7/Hand_Dyed_Silk_Noil.jpg/330px-Hand_Dyed_Silk_Noil.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Noil"},{"title":"Silk","blocks":[{"text":"Silk is a natural protein fiber, some forms of which can be woven or knitted into textiles. The protein fiber of silk is composed mainly of fibroin (75-80%) and a smaller coating of sericin (20-25%). It is most commonly produced from the cocoons of certain insect larvae. The best-known silk is obtained from the cocoons of the larvae of the mulberry silkworm Bombyx mori, which are reared in captivity (sericulture)."}],"image":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/2/2e/Silk_raw_01a.jpg/330px-Silk_raw_01a.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Silk"}],"images":[{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/8/8f/Meyers_b14_s0826a.jpg/500px-Meyers_b14_s0826a.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x","src":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/8/8f/Meyers_b14_s0826a.jpg/500px-Meyers_b14_s0826a.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail","alt":"Four of the most important domesticated silk moths. Top to bottom: B. mori, Hyalophora cecropia, Antheraea pernyi, Samia cynthia.From Meyers Konversations-Lexikon (1885–1892)","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/File%3AMeyers_b14_s0826a.jpg"},{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/e/ed/Eri_silk_worm.jpg/500px-Eri_silk_worm.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x, https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/e/ed/Eri_silk_worm.jpg/1280px-Eri_silk_worm.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 2x","src":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/e/ed/Eri_silk_worm.jpg/500px-Eri_silk_worm.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail","alt":"Rearing of wild Eri silk worm, Assam","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/File%3AEri_silk_worm.jpg"},{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/c/c1/Silk_from_Mawangdui_2.jpg/500px-Silk_from_Mawangdui_2.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x","src":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/c/c1/Silk_from_Mawangdui_2.jpg/500px-Silk_from_Mawangdui_2.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail","alt":"Woven silk textile from tomb no 1. at Mawangdui in China, from the Han dynasty, 2nd century BC","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/File%3ASilk_from_Mawangdui_2.jpg"},{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikiped